{
  "case_id": "2009-08-351",
  "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0000-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:03 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Paul Castiglione",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Despite the holding in Von Neumann that the civil forfeiture hearing itself is all the process that is due for the seizure of personal property, the Seventh Circuit struck down Illinois's Drug Asset Forfeiture Procedure Act specifically because it does not require an additional adversarial hearing that takes place post-seizure but before the forfeiture hearing."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: I -- I suppose, if the case is moot, then you are entitled to a Munsingwear order."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Paul Castiglione",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CASTIGLIONE: Well, maybe I can explain, and I think it also goes to the -- what is -- why the 8,850 -- the use of 8,850 and the Barker speedy trial factors makes sense. The one hearing where these issues are resolved is a civil forfeiture hearing, it's true. But I think what Slocum does, just in a similar way to how speedy trial factors operate, it gives the claimant an opportunity to come in and ask the court to tell the government to fish or cut bait."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So you're saying the answer\nto the question is there is no way? A person who doubts\nthis probable cause has no way. He has to wait 6 months\nuntil there's a forfeiture hearing. He's out of luck\nbecause then it merges with the merits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Castiglione",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CASTIGLIONE: It does merge with the\nmerits. That's historically --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: How do you get an\nexpedited hearing? I thought -- I thought for property\nunder 20,000, the State -- the time before the\nforfeiture proceeding could be 187 days."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Castiglione",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CASTIGLIONE: Forty-five of those days,\nYour Honor, is what would be the property owner deciding\nwhether to file a claim. And if -- I mean, one of the\npossibilities -- it\u2019s at section 6 of our statute, which\nis actually nonjudicial in rem forfeitures. If one were\nto simply file -- a property owner simply file a claim\nbut not a cash bond, then there wouldn't be a judicial\nproceeding. It would just be informal negotiations with\nthe State's attorney. One could always go to court by\nfiling a cash bond."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Well, what were they\nseeking to enjoin?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Jay",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. JAY: When the -- when the State shows\nprobable cause, the burden shifts to the claimant to\nprove by a preponderance -- so the ultimate burden is by\na preponderance."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I have two questions: Must it be an adversarial hearing, and must the State disprove the innocent owner defense?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Mr. Peters)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PETERS: No, what -- what I'm saying,\nYour Honor, is if we're -- if we're defining mootness as\nthe ultimate return of the property, then the property\nwas returned pursuant to settlements in four of the\ncases. The plaintiffs agreed, we\u2019ll pay $400 instead of\n$20,000, if you return our car. The car was returned.\nSo if the case was settled and became moot\nbecause of the settlement, it is not a situation in\nwhich the Court can adopt the Munsingwear position. It\nreally is a Bancorp situation, and the Seventh Circuit's\ndecision remains --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Mr. Castiglione)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CASTIGLIONE: One did, Your Honor, but\nit wasn't in response to the Federal litigation. So our\nposition would be if this is moot, this is really --\nMunsingwear would apply."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Well, what good would a\nMunsingwear order do, anyway? They'll just file another\nlawsuit, won't they?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Mr. Castiglione)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0003-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "is that the settlement wasn't in response. We didn't\nsettle the Federal -- we didn't settle the Federal\nlitigation. The -- the underlying forfeiture case was\n-- was resolved."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-351",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2c239d852f325020c62639dd32f10f248d7a34e5b484669ce3cb95f3a35ff63",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2c239d852f3-0003-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 11:09 a.m., the case in the\nabove-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
